A: ArrayList.Contains does not influence the count or position of elements, it merely verifies if the element is present. So there should be no change in the count and no change in position if the element is already present in the list. 
Here is the verification:


*

*The old count is equal to the new count as the Count property of list should remain the same.

*The index return by old IndexOf is not changed to the new IndexOf as it still refers to the same position, same applies to LastIndexOf.


ArrayList's Contains() method does not alter the current state of the object. Its purpose is simply to check if the object is in the collection. This can thus always return true, without changing the states of the object or collection, fulfilling the "reflexive" property.

To understand why "reflexive" property holds for Contains(), consider the mathematical concept of a relation - "if A is related to B, then B must be related to A". 
So assuming ArrayList A is related to an element B (i.e., B is in A), then B is also related to A (i.e., A contains B). 
And if A doesn't relate to B, then B can't relate to A (i.e., A doesn't contain B).
So Contains() is a "reflexive" method, it has the property where its result is always consistent, irrespective of the direction of the association.

If you want Contains() test to fail, you could test with a scenario where the element is not in the list - which will obviously return false (as expected) but if Contains() allows the count to change or position to change, it would fail this property. In which case you can use the Assert.IsFalse test method instead of Assert.IsTrue.

