A: Pex and Moles are tools for unit testing and they work very well together. However, they can introduce a layer of complexity for some types of tests, most notably ones involving collections, as they are not deterministic-like regular unit tests.

The issue you are experiencing could be a failure of one of these tools to correctly understand the behavior of the ArrayList.Contains() function. ArrayList.Contains() does not guarantee a particular order of contained elements, so if you call Contains() multiple times on the same list with the same value, it may give different results, because the order of the elements may change, causing the index found by IndexOf and LastIndexOf to seem "wrong".

Here are a few things you could try to mitigate the issue:

1. Be aware of the order of elements in the ArrayList when you work with Contains.

2. Consider using a different type for your testing collections. Some types, such as List<T>, have more straightforward rules for when elements can be considered "in" the collection.

3. If possible, consider refactoring your unit tests to use a different testing framework for collections, such as Moq or NSubstitute. These frameworks provide more predictability in terms of order of operations and mocking of the underlying objects.

4. It is possible you can't be bothered to refactor your unit tests at this time, and you could just use Pex and Moles together at your own peril. Be prepared that some unit tests could fail in subtle and hard-to-replicate ways. That's what a good test suite is supposed to do - fail when it can, and give you the information you need to fix the problem.
   
5. If you are really considering refactoring to a different testing framework, it might be a sign that you are having a difficult time testing your code. It might be time to re-evaluate whether you are actually testing your code correctly. Unit tests are to give you confidence that when you make a change, your existing tests will give you feedback on whether the change is breaking something. So, if you are spending a bunch of time trying to write tests that pass for code that isn't actually correctly implemented, it could be a sign that you need to work on your understanding of the problem and how to implement it correctly. 
 
Remember, in essence, you are trying to verify the correctness of Contains function, that Moles does not behave as expected. So your assertions should account for all possible return values. That is a bit tricky because you are testing the behavior of ArrayList.Contains, not the behavior of an individual object.

Hope this helps.

As a note: I, personally, would advise against using Moles for this scenario. Moles doesn't provide an extensive framework to test these kinds of scenarios. I would highly recommend revisiting the design of the unit test to use a different type of testing framework that better supports the specific behavior of the ArrayList.Contains method. Furthermore, I would recommend the use of a mocking framework (like MOQ), which will give you greater control over these concerns.

