A: The issue is that Pex doesn't support checking types that inherit from arbitrary classes, like ArrayList. This means that when running Pex tests, it can't check methods and properties that depend on the exact implementation of ArrayList (like .Add and ToArray methods). 
As a solution, you could create a wrapper class that mirrors the API of the ArrayList (or any other class) in your project and manually run tests on that instead. 
Another solution is to create your own contracts (as in your first example) that work with your specific implementation. Unfortunately, Pex and other testing tools don't support verifying code that uses methods or properties of arbitrary classes that inherit directly from System.Object (like ArrayList). You have to explicitly provide Pex with contracts for these types.
Sadly, the situation is not ideal and could change in future .Net testing tools. As a workaround you will have to create manual unit tests or third party tools specifically designed for .Net code verification.

A: Pex does not support testing against a custom implementation of classes or objects, including those that inherit from arbitrary classes. 

As an alternative solution, you can manually run your unit tests or leverage a tool specifically built for automated testing in .NET. One such tool is the Microsoft Fakes Framework, which allows you to isolate your unit tests from the .NET Framework and other external dependencies. It provides a way to test your code in a controlled environment, where the external dependencies can be replaced with mock objects. This way, you can create unit tests that are compatible with Pex. 

In conclusion, while it is true that Pex does not support testing against a custom implementation, there are alternative solutions available to achieve the desired results. One option is to leverage the Microsoft Fakes Framework for .NET, and the other option is to create manual unit tests.

I hope this helps in your search to get accurate and efficient results.

A: The use of Pex by Microsoft for .NET testing is intended for testing a class directly. It does not handle inheritance from generic or arbitrary base classes. Specifically it wouldn't be able to test methods on the base class if your tested class overrides them.

Since ArrayList doesn't follow these guidelines and methods in ArrayList aren’t covered by Pex, you either need to consider wrapping the ArrayList in an interface and testing that or use other tools like MS Fakes or manually test the List<T> version of the code. I recommend the first option as you would be able to test your specific functionality within these bounds.

In my opinion, testing the Microsoft's class directly with automated tool like Pex or other test tools can be intricate and often results in the limitations that you're experiencing. There is also a chance that, in future, these tools might not support testing of directly inherited or overridden methods.

A possible alternative solution would be creating the unit tests in a way that you control the instantiation and interaction of the classes under test and the mock objects it uses. It's not automatic testing like you get with Pex, but I believe it will give you the exact results you need.

This is a limitation of current .NET testing tools and a warning sign that your testing strategy might need adjustment to meet future maintenance requirements. 

I hope this helps. Let me know if I can assist with anything else.

(Disclaimer: This information is based on my understanding of the situation and might not be up to date with Microsoft's newest updates.)

A: As an alternative to the Pex, you can use the 'Moles' framework by Microsoft which is designed to make unit testing of .NET code easier and more manageable by allowing you to replace methods of classes in the .NET Framework. Moles replaces the methods of the class with a new one that records calls to the original method. You can call the base class's method from the overridden method.

But please note that Moles is not free tool. It is an expensive and heavy component.

Another good choice would be to create your own simple testing infrastructure that works only with your concrete class. You wouldn't test ArrayList in this case, but a custom List class that you control, so you can more easily design your tests and isolate the functionality of the class you're working on.

You shouldn't feel that there's no solution available when you look at third-party tools, for example 'moq', a popular mocking framework that allows you to test a separate part of your application by mocking its dependencies .

In conclusion, to test your ArrayList, you might have to create a wrapper around it where you can control dependency--------------------            bool Assert_LastIndexOf_x = New_Ret ? Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX : (New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);

