As you can see, the `ArrayListContractTest` partial class is decorated with two attributes in the `PUT_ContainsContract` method: one to specify the precondition (`[PexAssumeUnderTest]`) and another one to specify the invariants (`[PexInvariant]`).

The `[PexAssumeUnderTest]` attribute is used to specify assumptions for the test method. In the case of the `PUT_ContainsContract` method, these assumptions are:

- The ArrayList count before invoking the 'Contains' method is greater than or equal to 0.
- The item 'x' does not exist in the ArrayList before invoking the 'Contains' method.

The `[PexInvariant]` attribute is used to specify conditions that should be true whenever the code under test is invoked. The example provided does not contain any specific invariants, but they would be utilized to validate any assumptions or state maintained by the ArrayList class.

Keep in mind that you should add this data to the `GeneratedData` attribute for this example to be used by the Pex engine. If you want to test your partial classes, you should compile them into a DLL file and load it into Pex.

The `PUT_ContainsContract` test does not perform any checks itself, it is a contract test method. The generated tests will use these test methods to verify the behaviors of the tested code. If the tested code does not meet the conditions stated in the contracts, those tests will fail.

If you are not yet using Pex, you can easily enable it in Visual Studio 2010 by inserting an assembly reference to Pex.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting.dll. More information on using Pex can be found in the MSDN documentation here.

Remember, Pex is primarily a tool for generating automatically test data and for finding the faults in testable programs. It is also a good practice to write unit test methods that utilize your contracts wherever possible to ensure correct and robust testing.

Pex, of course, is useful only when used in conjunction with a full range of testing methodologies, such as code reviews, pair programming sessions, and continuous integration, among others.

